      {
        "id": "ev_passage_2961d70ac9b77749",
        "source_type": "passage",
        "provider": "direct_http",
        "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-024-08449-y",
        "document_id": null,
        "title": "Quantum error correction below the surface code threshold | Nature",
        "snippet": "Quantum error correction[1](#ref-CR1 \"Shor, P. W. Scheme for reducing decoherence in quantum computer memory. Phys. Rev. A 52, R2493 (1995).\"),[2](#ref-CR2 \"Gottesman, D. Stabilizer Codes and Quantum Error Correction (California Institute of Technology, 1997).\"),[3](#ref-CR3 \"Dennis, E., Kitaev, A. Y., Landahl, A. & Preskill, J. Topological quantum memory. J. Math. Phys. 43, 4452–4505 (2002).\"),[4](/articles/s41586-024-08449-y#ref-CR4 \"Kitaev, A. Y. Fault-tolerant quantum computation by anyons. Ann. Phys. 303, 2–30 (2003).\") provides a path to reach practical quantum computing by combining multiple physical qubits into a logical qubit, in which the logical error rate is suppressed exponentially as more qubits are added. However, this exponential suppression only occurs if the physical erro",
        "content_ref": "41c868d25655a716bab4a6d28c22ce663423453d243ba6e5d6d9346cb2410994",
        "published_date": null,
        "retrieved_at": "2026-05-04T17:19:38.414534+00:00",
        "query": null,
        "quality_score": null,
        "freshness_score": null,
        "citation_id": null,
        "metadata": {
