        "freshness_score": null,
        "citation_id": null,
        "metadata": {
          "start_char": 5706,
          "end_char": 6506,
          "heading": "[High-threshold and low-overhead fault-tolerant quantum memory](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-024-07107-7?fromPaywallRec=false)",
          "heading_path": [
            "[High-threshold and low-overhead fault-tolerant quantum memory](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-024-07107-7?fromPaywallRec=false)"
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "ev_passage_ac81aa2b1c5ff26e",
        "source_type": "passage",
        "provider": "direct_http",
        "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-024-08449-y",
        "document_id": null,
        "title": "Quantum error correction below the surface code threshold | Nature",
        "snippet": "for error-corrected surface code logical qubits[3](/articles/s41586-024-08449-y#ref-CR3 \"Dennis, E., Kitaev, A. Y., Landahl, A. & Preskill, J. Topological quantum memory. J. Math. Phys. 43, 4452–4505 (2002).\"),[4](/articles/s41586-024-08449-y#ref-CR4 \"Kitaev, A. Y. Fault-tolerant quantum computation by anyons. Ann. Phys. 303, 2–30 (2003).\"),[13](/articles/s41586-024-08449-y#ref-CR13 \"Fowler, A. G., Mariantoni, M., Martinis, J. M. & Cleland, A. N. Surface codes: towards practical large-scale quantum computation. Phys. Rev. A 86, 032324 (2012).\"). Here *d* is the code distance indicating 2*d*2 − 1 physical qubits used per logical qubit; *p* and *ε**d* are the physical and logical error rates, respectively; and *p*thr is the threshold error rate of the code. Thus, when *p* ≪ *p*thr, the error",
        "content_ref": "65941b0f95f5655e48ed9ada46979127dbcac4723d643674d806072e0e495d50",
        "published_date": null,
        "retrieved_at": "2026-05-04T17:19:38.414534+00:00",
        "query": null,
        "quality_score": null,
        "freshness_score": null,
        "citation_id": null,
        "metadata": {
          "start_char": 8090,
          "end_char": 8890,
          "heading": "[High-threshold and low-overhead fault-tolerant quantum memory](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-024-07107-7?fromPaywallRec=false)",
          "heading_path": [
            "[High-threshold and low-overhead fault-tolerant quantum memory](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-024-07107-7?fromPaywallRec=false)"
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "ev_passage_e5a1f435125770cf",
        "source_type": "passage",
        "provider": "direct_http",
        "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-024-08449-y",
        "document_id": null,
        "title": "Quantum error correction below the surface code threshold | Nature",
        "snippet": "We begin with results from our 105-qubit Willow processor depicted in Fig. [1a](/articles/s41586-024-08449-y#Fig1). It features a square grid of superconducting transmon qubits[24](/articles/s41586-024-08449-y#ref-CR24 \"Koch, J. et al. Charge-insensitive qubit design derived from the Cooper pair box. Phys. Rev. A 76, 042319 (2007).\") with improved operational fidelities compared with our previously reported Sycamore processors[17](/articles/s41586-024-08449-y#ref-CR17 \"Google Quantum AI. Suppressing quantum errors by scaling a surface code logical qubit. Nature 614, 676–681 (2023).\"),[25](/articles/s41586-024-08449-y#ref-CR25 \"Arute, F. et al. Quantum supremacy using a programmable superconducting processor. Nature 574, 505–510 (2019).\"). The qubits have a mean coherence time *T*1 of 68 μs",
        "content_ref": "fde99349fb0414211edf67500ee95ab64683409e6911ef8361cefb4e8d03a381",
        "published_date": null,
        "retrieved_at": "2026-05-04T17:19:38.414534+00:00",
        "query": null,
        "quality_score": null,
        "freshness_score": null,
        "citation_id": null,
        "metadata": {
          "start_char": 12760,
          "end_char": 13560,
          "heading": "[High-threshold and low-overhead fault-tolerant quantum memory](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-024-07107-7?fromPaywallRec=false)",
          "heading_path": [
            "[High-threshold and low-overhead fault-tolerant quantum memory](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-024-07107-7?fromPaywallRec=false)"
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "ev_passage_79a65ba3e9302358",
        "source_type": "passage",
        "provider": "direct_http",
        "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-024-08449-y",
        "document_id": null,
        "title": "Quantum error correction below the surface code threshold | Nature",
        "snippet": "We operate a distance-7 surface code memory comprising 49 data qubits, 48 measure qubits and 4 additional leakage removal qubits[17](/articles/s41586-024-08449-y#ref-CR17 \"Google Quantum AI. Suppressing quantum errors by scaling a surface code logical qubit. Nature 614, 676–681 (2023).\"). Summarizing, we initiate surface code operation by preparing the data qubits in a product state corresponding to a logical eigenstate of either the *X*L or *Z*L basis of the *ZXXZ* surface code[31](/articles/s41586-024-08449-y#ref-CR31 \"Bonilla Ataides, J. P., Tuckett, D. K., Bartlett, S. D., Flammia, S. T. & Brown, B. J. The XZZX surface code. Nat Commun. 12, 2172 (2021).\"). We then repeat a variable number of cycles of error correction, during which the measured qubits extract parity information from th",
        "content_ref": "e9cd131d51ba1c6d464dccdba9f98a93c444b580929c9e22be82943b038c8405",
        "published_date": null,
        "retrieved_at": "2026-05-04T17:19:38.414534+00:00",
        "query": null,
        "quality_score": null,
        "freshness_score": null,
        "citation_id": null,
        "metadata": {
          "start_char": 17904,
          "end_char": 18704,
          "heading": "[High-threshold and low-overhead fault-tolerant quantum memory](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-024-07107-7?fromPaywallRec=false)",
          "heading_path": [
            "[High-threshold and low-overhead fault-tolerant quantum memory](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-024-07107-7?fromPaywallRec=false)"
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "ev_passage_7972a689a38a1b4b",
        "source_type": "passage",
        "provider": "direct_http",
        "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-024-08449-y",
        "document_id": null,
        "title": "Quantum error correction below the surface code threshold | Nature",
        "snippet": "e data qubits to be sent to the decoder. After each syndrome extraction, we run data qubit leakage removal (DQLR)[32](/articles/s41586-024-08449-y#ref-CR32 \"Miao, K. C. et al. Overcoming leakage in quantum error correction. Nat. Phys. 19, 1780–1786 (2023).\") to ensure that leakage to higher states is short-lived. We measure the state of the logical qubit by measuring the individual data qubits and then check whether the corrected logical measurement outcome of the decoder agrees with the initial logical state. It is worth noting that fault-tolerant computation does not require active correction of the code state; the decoder can simply reinterpret the logical measurement outcomes[13](/articles/s41586-024-08449-y#ref-CR13 \"Fowler, A. G., Mariantoni, M., Martinis, J. M. & Cleland, A. N. Surf",
        "content_ref": "761c4565fcfbe3f385bbdc4255878723a7fe7918e092471d83d62a2956a4a2ce",
        "published_date": null,
        "retrieved_at": "2026-05-04T17:19:38.414534+00:00",
        "query": null,
        "quality_score": null,
        "freshness_score": null,
        "citation_id": null,
        "metadata": {
