ra Johnson-Groh](https://www.sciencenews.org/author/mara-johnson-groh \"Posts by Mara Johnson-Groh\")April 16, 2026\n* [Space](https://www.sciencenews.org/topic/space) \n  + [Space](https://www.sciencenews.org/topic/space) \n    - [Astronomy](https://www.sciencenews.org/topic/astronomy)\n    - [Planetary Science](https://www.sciencenews.org/topic/planetary-science)\n    - [Cosmology](https://www.sciencenews.org/topic/cosmology)\n  + Recent posts in Space\n    ---------------------\n\n    - [Planetary Science](https://www.sciencenews.org/topic/planetary-science) \n\n\n      ### [A small object past Pluto may have a thin atmosphere](https://www.sciencenews.org/article/atmosphere-small-object-past-pluto)\n\n      By [Lisa Grossman](https://www.sciencenews.org/author/lisa-grossman \"Posts by Lisa Grossman\")2 hours ago\n    - [Planetary Science](https://www.sciencenews.org/topic/planetary-science) \n\n\n      ### [Uranus has weird rings. Astronomers now know the source of two of them](https://www.sciencenews.org/article/uranus-rings-origin-mu-moon-mab)\n\n      By [Robin George Andrews](https://www.sciencenews.org/author/robin-george-andrews \"Posts by Robin George Andrews\")April 28, 2026\n    - [Astronomy](https://www.sciencenews.org/topic/astronomy) \n\n\n      ### [The earliest evidence of the first stars may lie in a distant gas clump](https://www.sciencenews.org/article/first-stars-earliest-evidence-universe)\n\n      By [Mara Johnson-Groh](https://www.sciencenews.org/author/mara-johnson-groh \"Posts by Mara Johnson-Groh\")April 24, 2026\n\n\n\n[News](https://www.sciencenews.org/article-type/news) \n\n[Quantum Physics](https://www.sciencenews.org/topic/quantum-physics)\n\nA quantum computer corrected its own errors, improving its calculations\n=======================================================================\n\nIt’s a step in the right direction for reliable quantum computing\n\n![A rainbow-hued quantum computing processor](https://www.sciencenews.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2024/09/090924_ec_quantum_feat.jpg?w=1030)\n\nScientists used Quantinuum’s H2 quantum computer (chip shown) to demonstrate a new milestone in quantum error correction.\n\nBy [Emily Conover](https://www.sciencenews.org/author/emily-conover \"Posts by Emily Conover\")\n\nSeptember 10, 2024 at 9:00 am\n\nShare this:\n-----------\n\n* [Share](#)\n\n* [Share via email (Opens in new window)\n  Email](mailto:?subject=A%20quantum%20computer%20corrected%20its%20own%20errors%2C%20improving%20its%20calculations%C2%A0&body=I%20saw%20this%20on%20Science%20News%3A%20https%3A%2F%2Fwww.sciencenews.org%2Farticle%2Fquantum-computer-error-correction%3Futm_source%3Dinternal%26utm_medium%3Demail%26utm_campaign%3Demail_share)\n* [Share on Facebook (Opens in new window)\n  Facebook](https://www.sciencenews.org/article/quantum-computer-error-correction?share=facebook)\n* [Share on Reddit (Opens in new window)\n  Reddit](https://www.sciencenews.org/article/quantum-computer-error-correction?share=reddit)\n* [Share on X (Opens in new window)\n  X](https://www.sciencenews.org/article/quantum-computer-error-correction?share=twitter)\n* [Print (Opens in new window)\n  Print](https://www.sciencenews.org/article/quantum-computer-error-correction#print?share=print)\n\nFor the first time, a quantum computer has improved its results by repeatedly fixing its own mistakes midcalculation with a technique called quantum error correction.\n\nScientists have long known that quantum computers [need error correction](https://www.sciencenews.org/article/quantum-computers-hype-supremacy-error-correction-problems) to meet their potential to solve problems that stump standard, “classical” computers (*SN: 6/22/20*). Quantum computers calculate with quantum bits, or qubits, which are subject to quantum physics but suffer from jitters that result in mistakes.\n\nIn quantum error correction, multiple faulty qubits are combined to make reliable qubits, called logical qubits, which are then used to perform the calculation. Previous efforts found that error correction made calculations worse, rather than better, or detected errors but [didn’t actually fix them](https://www.sciencenews.org/article/quantum-computer-error-correction-multiple-qubits-detect-mistakes) (*SN: 10/4/21*).\n\n### Sign up for our newsletter\n\nWe summarize the week's scientific breakthroughs every Thursday.\n\nNow, scientists have performed repeated rounds of operations and error correction on eight logical qubits, researchers from Microsoft and the quantum computing company Quantinuum [report September 10 at the Quantum World Congress](https://events.registr8.com/catalog/assets/attendee/3A1BACCA-B528-453C-A453-C1390BF3EA19/catalog) in Tysons, Va., and i[n a paper posted online at arXiv.org](https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.04628). The operations performed in the calculation imbued the qubits with correlations called quantum entanglement. The corrected calculation had an error rate about a tenth that of one performed with the original, error-prone qubits, which are called “physical” qubits.\n\nThe researchers also entangled 12 logical qubits, the largest number of logical qubits ever entangled. The error rate for this entangled state was less than one-twentieth that of the equivalent state achieved using the computer’s initial faulty, physical qubits.\n\n“Error correction is working; this is huge,” says computer scientist Krysta Svore of Microsoft. “This is the direction we need to go for reliable quantum computing.”\n\nThe researchers used a quantum computer developed by the company Quantinuum, with 56 qubits made from electrically charged atoms, or ions. Those qubits were combined to make the logical qubits.\n\nFor correcting errors, a variety of schemes exist, and each one can fix a certain number of mistakes. The device in the study used an error correction scheme that was able to fix only one error. If the computer made two errors, the researchers were unable to fix the mistake, and they instead detected the mistakes and threw the result away to avoid inaccurate results.\n\nIn another recent milestone, researchers from Google reported August 24 at arXiv.org that error correction [increases the length of time a qubit can store information in memory](https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.13687), though the team didn’t perform calculations with it. Taken together, the Microsoft and Google results are “showing that error correction works like we expect,” says Ken Brown, a quantum engineer at Duke University and a scientific adviser for the quantum computing company IonQ. “That’s really promising.”\n\nBut more improvements are needed. The Microsoft result falls short of demonstrating a universal quantum computer, one that can perform all the operations that a quantum computer is capable of. “That’s the next big challenge, is getting enough resources … that you can actually do full universal quantum computing on many logical qubits,” Brown says.\n\nIn another study, Microsoft researchers combined high-performance classical computing, artificial intelligence and quantum computing to [perform a chemistry calculation](https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.05835). The calculation can be done without a quantum computer, but the study was a proof of concept. The calculation used two logical qubits, and the researchers found that the results were improved compared with a calculation performed with the error-prone physical qubits.\n\nIn the future, when quantum computers have more logical qubits, such chemistry calculations could unlock secrets that classical computers can’t access. Scientists hope the machines might reveal how to make fertilizer more efficiently, or how to extract carbon out of the atmosphere to combat global warming. “At the core, we want to save and feed our planet,” Svore says.\n\n\n\nQuestions or comments on this article? E-mail us at [feedback@sciencenews.org](mailto:feedback@sciencenews.org) | [Reprints FAQ](https://www.sciencenews.org/permission-republish)\n\n### Editor's Note:\n\nThis story was updated on September 10, 2024, to clarify Ken Brown's role at IonQ.\n\nA version of this article appears in the [October 5, 2024](https://www.sciencenews.org/sn-magazine/october-5-2024) issue of Science News.\n\n### Citations\n\nR. Hazra. [Industry Perspective: Quantinuum](https://events.registr8.com/catalog/assets/attendee/3A1BACCA-B528-453C-A453-C1390BF3EA19/catalog). Quantum World Congress, Tysons, Va., September 10, 2024.\n\nJ. Zander and K. Svore. [Industry Perspective: Microsoft](https://events.registr8.com/catalog/assets/attendee/3A1BACCA-B528-453C-A453-C1390BF3EA19/catalog). Quantum World Congress, Tysons, Va., September 10, 2024.\n\nB.W. Reichardt *et al*. [Demonstration of quantum computation and error correction with a tesseract code](https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.04628). arXiv:2409.04628. Submitted on September 6, 2024.\n\nMicrosoft Azure Quantum. [End-to-end quantum simulation of a chemical system](https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.05835). arXiv:2409.05835. Submitted on September 9, 2024.\n\nR. Acharya *et al.*[Quantum error correction below the surface code threshold](https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.13687). arXiv:2408.13687. Submitted August 24, 2024.\n\n[![Emily Conover](https://www.sciencenews.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2019/08/e_conover.jpg?w=80&h=80&crop=1)](https://www.sciencenews.org/author/emily-conover) \n\n### About [Emily Conover](https://www.sciencenews.org/author/emily-conover \"Posts by Emily Conover\")\n\n* [E-mail](mailto:econover@sciencenews.org)\n* [X](https://x.com/emcconover)\n\nSenior physics writer Emily Conover has a Ph.D. in physics from the University of Chicago. She is a two-time winner of the D.C. Science Writers’ Association Newsbrief award and a winner of the Acoustical Society of America’s Science Communication Award.\n\nWe are at a critical time and **supporting climate journalism is more important than ever.** Science News and our parent organization, the Society for Science, need your help to st