ne.svg)\n\nStore](https://quera-store.com/)[![](https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/643b94c382e84463a9e52264/68ae9e9ee26f59825c8b3c75_Contact%20Us.svg)\n\nContact Us](/contact-us)[![](https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/643b94c382e84463a9e52264/69940a9460a1970bfc2018a0_ask%20ai.svg)\n\nAsk AI about QuEra](/ask-ai-about-quera)\n\nContact Information\n\nPhone: +1 617-588-7207  \nEmail: info@quera.com\n\nOffice Location\n\n1380 Soldiers Field Road,  \nBoston, MA 02135, USA\n\n[Contact us](/contact-us)\n\n[Contact us](/contact-us)\n\n![](https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/643b94c382e84463a9e52264/68aeb040d72450ddb9da53a1_material-symbols_menu-rounded.svg)![](https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/643b94c382e84463a9e52264/68aebdd5d772eb01bc3bf652_material-symbols_close-rounded.svg)\n\nSearch\n\n![x icon\n](https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/643b94c382e84463a9e52264/6440f429532729b54e578345_x%20icon.svg)\n\nApril 28, 2026\n\nQuantum computing is coming this decade. Whether it is ‘good’ is up to us\n\nhttps://www.node-magazine.com/thoughtleadership/quantum-computing-is-coming-this-decade-whether-it-is-good-is-up-to-us\n\n57th Annual Meeting of the APS Division of Atomic, Molecular and Optical Physics\n\nFrom Assembly to Abstraction: The Quantum BASIC Moment\n\n[![arrow left](https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/643b94c382e84463a9e52264/643f8ddf07be6036b8bebf07_angle-right.svg)\n\nBack to all](/old/blog)\n\nError Correction and Fault Tolerance: The Engineering Sprint Toward Reliable Quantum Computing\n==============================================================================================\n\n![calender icon](https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/643b94c382e84463a9e52264/643f906c66296ab3742d65df_calendar.svg)\n\nFebruary 14, 2026\n\n![clock icon](https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/643b94c382e84463a9e52264/643f906c3befe842380987a5_clock.svg)\n\nmin read\n\nTechnology\n\n![share icon](https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/643b94c382e84463a9e52264/643f906cde8f6e5543cd4e23_share.svg)![share icon](https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/643b94c382e84463a9e52264/643fee008f7063114c44dfc2_share.svg)\n\nShare\n\n[![twitter icon](https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/643b94c382e84463a9e52264/643fedf8a280c7d0b577b012_twitter.svg)](#)[![Linkedin icon](https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/643b94c382e84463a9e52264/643fedf84e53a2730126211d_linkedin.svg)](#)[![copy to clipboard icon](https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/643b94c382e84463a9e52264/643fedf84debf06802d50edf_link.svg)\n\nCopied!](#)\n\n![](https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/643d2eea03135260bdaca209/6990b3a28876bdf09abbd296_Error%20Correction%20and%20Fault%20Tolerance.webp)\n\nWhy This Matters To HPC Centers\n-------------------------------\n\nHPC centers live and die by reliability. Classical supercomputers deliver reproducible, verifiable results. Quantum error correction (QEC) is what bridges the gap between noisy quantum experiments and the kind of dependable computation HPC users demand. The recent burst of QEC breakthroughs represents a shift from proof-of-concept demonstrations to scalable engineering approaches that directly affect timelines for when quantum resources become genuinely useful in production HPC environments.\n\n**The Evidence Base**\n---------------------\n\n▸ “Quantum computers will finally be useful: what’s behind the revolution” ([Nature](https://www.nature.com/articles/d41586-026-00312-6))\n\n▸ \"Quantum Simulations Become 100x More Efficient With New Error Bound\" ([Quantum Zeitgeist](https://quantumzeitgeist.com/quantum-error-simulations-become-100times-more-efficient/))\n\n▸ “Understanding Fault-tolerant Quantum Computing” ([QuEra](https://www.quera.com/blog-posts/understanding-fault-tolerant-quantum-computing))\n\n▸ \"Researchers Demonstrate Fault-Tolerant Quantum Operations Using Lattice Surgery\" ([Quantum Computing Report](https://quantumcomputingreport.com/paul-scherrer-institute-and-eth-zurich-demonstrate-fault-tolerant-lattice-surgery-on-superconducting-qubits/))\n\n▸ \"QuEra Unveils Breakthrough in Algorithmic Fault Tolerance\" ([QuEra](https://www.quera.com/press-releases/quera-and-collaborators-unveil-breakthrough-in-algorithmic-fault-tolerance-for-quantum-computing-cutting-runtime-overheads-and-accelerating-the-path-to-real-world-applications))\n\n▸ \"Paul Scherrer Institute and ETH Zurich Demonstrate Fault-Tolerant Lattice Surgery\" ([QCR](https://quantumcomputingreport.com/paul-scherrer-institute-and-eth-zurich-demonstrate-fault-tolerant-lattice-surgery-on-superconducting-qubits/))\n\n**Details**\n-----------\n\nQuantum error correction (QEC) has long been the field's most critical bottleneck. Multiple independent research groups have demonstrated fault-tolerant operations, new QRC code designs, and efficiency breakthroughs that compress the resource overhead required for reliable quantum computation. For HPC centers, these advances directly affect planning timelines: fault-tolerant quantum computing is becoming an engineering problem with measurable, accelerating progress.\n\nThe lattice surgery demonstrations from PSI/ETH Zurich and from superconducting qubit teams represent an important milestone. Lattice surgery is a candidate technique for performing logical operations between error-corrected qubits at scale, and successful demonstrations on real hardware validate the theoretical frameworks that quantum computing roadmaps depend on. Meanwhile, new error bound techniques have achieved 100x efficiency improvements in quantum simulation. This is the kind of step-change that reshapes what is computationally feasible.\n\nFor HPC centers, the practical takeaway is that the path to useful quantum computation runs through error correction, and that path is being paved faster than many expected. Advances in qLDPC codes, bosonic codes, phantom codes, and improved decoders all contribute to reducing the physical-to-logical qubit overhead. This, in turn, reduces the scale of quantum hardware needed to tackle real scientific problems. Centers should be tracking these developments closely because they directly determine when quantum will cross the threshold from \"interesting experiment\" to \"production tool.\"\n\nThe diversity of QEC approaches also matters strategically. HPC centers do not need to bet on a single error correction paradigm. But they do need staff who understand the landscape well enough to evaluate vendor claims and assess when quantum resources will be reliable enough to integrate into mission-critical workflows.\n\nLearn more about QuEra’s Quantum Error Correction approach [here](https://www.quera.com/quantum-error-correction).\n\n‍\n\n### Learn more about machine learning with QuEra\n\n[Listen to the podcast](#)\n\nNo items found.\n\n### See also\n\n[![](https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/643d2eea03135260bdaca209/69e9393ad30bc46af4526cca_Quantum%20Computing%20and%20Financial%20Services_%20Strategic%20Risk%2C%20Emerging%20Opportunity.jpg)\n\nOpinion\n\nQuantum Computing for Financial Services: Two Clocks Are Running\n\nApril 23, 2026\n\nmin read](/blog-posts/quantum-computing-for-financial-services-two-clocks-are-running)\n\n[![](https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/643d2eea03135260bdaca209/6921ebc270cc3376da4bd9e6_dean-personal-blog.webp)\n\nOpinion\n\nFrom Assembly to Abstraction: The Quantum BASIC Moment\n\nApril 22, 2026\n\nmin read](/blog-posts/from-assembly-to-abstraction-the-quantum-basic-moment)\n\n[![](https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/643d2eea03135260bdaca209/69e66178d64b64fc868bca10_UHR%20Launch.png)\n\nTechnology\n\nQuantum Error Correction at Record Efficiency: Why Neutral Atoms Are Leading the Way\n\nApril 20, 2026\n\nmin read](/blog-posts/quantum-error-correction-at-record-efficiency-why-neutral-atoms-are-leading-the-way)\n\n[Back to all](/blog)\n\nTechnology\n\nError Correction and Fault Tolerance: The Engineering Sprint Toward Reliable Quantum Computing\n==============================================================================================\n\nFebruary 14, 2026\n\nmin read\n\n6 min read\n\n![](https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/643d2eea03135260bdaca209/6990b3a28876bdf09abbd296_Error%20Correction%20and%20Fault%20Tolerance.webp)\n\n![Abstract background with white center and soft gradient corners in purple and orange with dotted patterns.](https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/643b94c382e84463a9e52264/6902334027dc5bf813610a44_Bg-glossary-inner-2x%20(1).webp)\n\nWhy This Matters To HPC Centers\n-------------------------------\n\nHPC centers live and die by reliability. Classical supercomputers deliver reproducible, verifiable results. Quantum error correction (QEC) is what bridges the gap between noisy quantum experiments and the kind of dependable computation HPC users demand. The recent burst of QEC breakthroughs represents a shift from proof-of-concept demonstrations to scalable engineering approaches that directly affect timelines for when quantum resources become genuinely useful in production HPC environments.\n\n**The Evidence Base**\n---------------------\n\n▸ “Quantum computers will finally be useful: what’s behind the revolution” ([Nature](https://www.nature.com/articles/d41586-026-00312-6))\n\n▸ \"Quantum Simulations Become 100x More Efficient With New Error Bound\" ([Quantum Zeitgeist](https://quantumzeitgeist.com/quantum-error-simulations-become-100times-more-efficient/))\n\n▸ “Understanding Fault-tolerant Quantum Computing” ([QuEra](https://www.quera.com/blog-posts/understanding-fault-tolerant-quantum-computing))\n\n▸ \"Researchers Demonstrate Fault-Tolerant Quantum Operations Using Lattice Surgery\" ([Quantum Computing Report](https://quantumcomputingreport.com/paul-scherrer-institute-and-eth-zurich-demonstrate-fault-tolerant-lattice-surgery-on-superconducting-qubits/))\n\n▸ \"QuEra Unveils Breakthrough in Algorithmic Fault Tolerance\" ([QuEra](https://www.quera.com/press-releases/quera-and-collaborators-unveil-breakthrough-in-algorithmic-fault-tolerance-for-quantum-computing-cutting-runtime-overheads-and-accelerating-the-path-to-real-world-applications))\n\n▸ \"Paul Scherrer Institute and ETH Zurich Demonstrate Fault-Tolerant Lattice Surgery\" ([QCR](https://quantumcomputingreport.com/paul-scherrer-institute-and-eth-zurich-demonstrate-fault-tolerant-lattice-surgery-on-superconducting-qubits/))\n\n**Details**\n-----------\n\nQuantum error correction (QEC) has long been the field's most critical bottleneck. Multiple independent research groups have demonstrated fault-tolerant operations, new QRC code designs, and efficiency breakthroughs that compress the resource overhead required for reliable quantum computation. For HPC centers, these advances directly affect planning timelines: fault-tolerant quantum computing is becoming an engineering problem with measurable, accelerating progress.\n\nThe lattice surgery demonstrations from PSI/ETH Zurich and from superconducting qubit teams represent an important milestone. Lattice surgery is a candidate technique for performing logical operations between error-corrected qubits at scale, and successful demonstrations on real hardware validate the theoretical frameworks that quantum computing roadmaps depend on. Meanwhile, new error bound techniques have achieved 100x efficiency improvements in quantum simulation. This is the kind of step-change that reshapes what is computationally feasible.\n\nFor HPC centers, the practical takeaway is that the path to useful quantum computation runs through error correction, and that path is being paved faster than many expected. Advances in qLDPC codes, bosonic codes, phantom codes, and improved decoders all contribute to reducing the physical-to-logical qubit overhead. This, in turn, reduces the scale of quantum hardware needed to tackle real scientific problems. Centers should be tracking these developments closely because they directly determine when quantum will cross the threshold from \"interesting experiment\" to \"production tool.\"\n\nThe diversity of QEC approaches also matters strategically. HPC centers do not need to bet on a single error correction paradigm. But they do need staff who understand the landscape well enough to evaluate vendor claims and assess when quantum resources will be reliable enough to integrate into mission-critical workflows.\n\nLearn more about QuEra’s Quantum Error Correction approach [here](https://www.quera.com/quantum-error-correction).\n\n‍\n\n### Learn more about machine learning with QuEra\n\n[Listen to the podcast](#)\n\nNo items found.\n\n### Table of contents\n\nThis is some text inside of a div block.\n\nShare\n\n[Copied!](#)\n\nContinue reading\n----------------\n\n[Technology\n\n![](https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/643d2eea03135260bdaca209/69837028c107f68d1cf6bbc3_Trevor-tutorial.webp)\n\nFebruary 3, 2026\n\nNeutral Atoms and the Path to Fault-Tolerant Quantum Computing](/blog-posts/neutral-atoms-and-the-path-to-fault-tolerant-quantum-computing)\n\n[Opinion\n\n![](https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/643d2eea03135260bdaca209/65a81a077a9cd7dc9b679ca3_Banner_template-Dec-22.webp)\n\nDecember 15, 2022\n\nInsights from the Quantum Era - December 2022](/blog-posts/insights-from-the-quantum-era---december-2022)\n\n[Technology\n\n![](https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/643d2eea03135260bdaca209/6990b2b46ce16e41d5e5fbae_The%20Hybrid%20Quantum-HPC%20Architecture%20Is%20Taking%20Shape.webp)\n\nFebruary 14, 2026\n\nThe Hybrid Quantum-HPC Architecture Is Taking Shape](/blog-posts/the-hybrid-quantum-hpc-architecture-is-taking-shape)\n\n![](https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/643b94c382e84463a9e52264/68ac4c2ef923280e98cf80e0_footer%20blue.svg)![](https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/643b94c382e84463a9e52264/68ac4c2eef815dfbea19f257_footer%20gradeitn.svg)\n\n![Abstract gradient background with smooth blend of purple, pink, and orange hues.](https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/643b94c382e84463a9e52264/68bd3b0a13de4e5bad3bf547_7685652771fe9138412e4a901ebdef89_footer%20mobile%20bg.png)\n\nSign up for our newsletters\n-----------