        "method": "direct_http",
        "quote": "[*![](https://img.gmw.cn/images/51990.files/images/002.png)*](javascript:void(0); \"Twitter\") [*![](https://img.gmw.cn/images/51990.files/images/003.png)*](# \"Reddit\") *![](https://img.gmw.cn/images/51990.files/images/004.png?v=1)*X [*![](ht",
        "snippet_hash": "120891b59dea68ceaac6de95787801dc46a82911d154f46699ec27a585e9cb81"
      },
      {
        "url": "https://en.gmw.cn/2023-03/27/content_36457700.htm",
        "text": "Led by Yu Dapeng, an academician with the Chinese Academy of Sciences, the team extended the storage time of quantum information beyond the break-even point for the first time through repetitive real-time QEC. The findings were published online in the journal Nature.\n\nAccording to Xu Yuan, an assistant researcher at the Southern University of Science and Technology, although quantum information processing based on superconducting quantum circuit system has made great progress in recent years, the error rate of quantum operations is still much higher than that of a classical computer. Therefore, QEC, which protects logical qubits from noises, is extremely important.",
        "start_char": 1913,
        "end_char": 2586,
        "heading": null,
        "heading_path": null,
        "page_title": "Chinese scientists achieve breakthrough in quantum error correction _Guangming Online",
        "retrieved_at": "2026-05-04T17:19:33.438111+00:00",
        "method": "direct_http",
        "quote": "Led by Yu Dapeng, an academician with the Chinese Academy of Sciences, the team extended the storage time of quantum information beyond the break-even point for the first time through repetitive real-time QEC. The findings were published on",
        "snippet_hash": "537ffa1f21c4231d40e34c9056fc57595db1990c38dfedc1228fa09543efde9b"
      },
      {
        "url": "https://en.gmw.cn/2023-03/27/content_36457700.htm",
        "text": "Conventional QEC schemes use multiple physical qubits to encode a logical qubit, which not only requires a great number of hardware resources but also adds the number of error channels with increased number of physical qubits, resulting in an awkward situation of \"more corrections, more errors.\"\n\nThis problem has not yet been solved to achieve a positive QEC gain to reach the break-even point, which is defined as the lifetime of the best available physical component in this system.\n\nIn this study, the research team developed a quantum system with high coherence performance, implemented an error syndrome detection method with low error rates, and improved the QEC procedure.",
        "start_char": 2588,
        "end_char": 3269,
        "heading": null,
        "heading_path": null,
        "page_title": "Chinese scientists achieve breakthrough in quantum error correction _Guangming Online",
